## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of dynamic plugin architectures, runtime extensibility, and robust command dispatch with isolation. It also probes conflict resolution and aliasing decisions in a real toolchain.

## Key Concepts
- Plugin Registry
- Lazy initialization and sandboxed plugins
- Command dispatch and argv parsing
- Aliases and version-aware routing
- Conflict resolution and fallback behavior

## Code Example
```typescript
export interface Command {
  name: string
  run(args: string[]): Promise<void>
}
export interface PluginFactory {
  create(): Command
  version: string
}
export interface Registry {
  register(name: string, factory: PluginFactory): void
  get(name: string): Command | undefined
  resolveAlias(name: string): string | undefined
}
```

```typescript
// usage (pseudo)
const registry: Registry = createRegistry();
registry.register('deploy', { create: () => new DeployCommand(), version: '1.0.3' });
const cmdName = process.argv[2];
const cmd = registry.get(cmdName) || registry.get(registry.resolveAlias(cmdName) ?? '');
cmd?.run(process.argv.slice(3));
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test plugin isolation and error containment?
- How would you hot-swap plugins in a long-running process without restart?

